{"w": 595.3200073242188, "h": 809.0257568359375, "lines": [{"t": "12", "x": 0.9086877902929988, "y": 0.9742087522985531, "w": 0.025055458310636873, "h": 0.017428414981790374, "c": 1, "size": 12.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "Before humans arrived, kākāpō were common throughout New Zealand's forests.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.12319506908974809, "w": 0.8266068505328683, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "However, this all changed with the arrival of the first Polynesian settlers about", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.15152541916388573, "w": 0.8257508696935519, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "700 years ago. For the early settlers, the flightless kākāpō was easy prey. They", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.17970752416545568, "w": 0.8266820013240073, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "ate its meat and used its feathers to make soft cloaks. With them came the", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.2080378742395933, "w": 0.8266594458341702, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "Polynesian dog and rat, which also preyed on kakap6. By the time European", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.23636822431373095, "w": 0.8260336335161456, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "colonisers arrived in the early 1800s, kākāpō had become confined to the central", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.26469857438786853, "w": 0.8263786299856988, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "North Island and forested parts of the South Island. The fall in kākāpō numbers", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.2930660423122369, "w": 0.8267844237528583, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "was accelerated by European colonisation. A great deal of habitat was lost", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.32139637352567824, "w": 0.8260960712130128, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "through forest clearance, and introduced species such as deer depleted the", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.3497267990426009, "w": 0.8259249570651125, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "remaining forests of food. Other predators such as cats, stoats and two more", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.3780571491167385, "w": 0.8260336335161456, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "species of rat were also introduced. The kākāpō were in serious trouble.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.40638753691226864, "w": 0.7312794560600091, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "In 1894, the New Zealand government launched its first attempt to save the", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.4630482747819364, "w": 0.8260552662813984, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "kākāpō. Conservationist Richard Henry led an effort to relocate several hundred", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.49140337008956114, "w": 0.8260303527176238, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "of the birds to predator-free Resolution Island in Fiordland. Unfortunately, the", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5197337201636988, "w": 0.8267279325033118, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "island didn't remain predator free - stoats arrived within six years, eventually", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5480641079592289, "w": 0.8264489621040089, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "destroying the kākāpō population. By the mid-1900s, the kākāpō was practically a", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.5763944580333665, "w": 0.8267415683221678, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "lost species. Only a few clung to life in the most isolated parts of New Zealand.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6047248458288966, "w": 0.8052900671876779, "h": 0.01882263537038409, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "From 1949 to 1973, the newly formed New Zealand Wildlife Service made over 60", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6612743433120499, "w": 0.8260771040965588, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "expeditions to find kākāpō, focusing mainly on Fiordland. Six were caught, but", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.6896046933861876, "w": 0.8262720040337418, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "there were no females amongst them and all but one died within a few months of", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7179351189031102, "w": 0.8260553688063522, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "captivity. In 1974, a new initiative was launched, and by 1977, 18 more kākāpō were", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7462654689772478, "w": 0.8267853464774425, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "found in Fiordland. However, there were still no females. In 1977, a large", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.7745958190513854, "w": 0.8257507671685981, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "population of males was spotted in Rakiura - a large island free from stoats,", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8029261691255231, "w": 0.8268093373166329, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "ferrets and weasels. There were about 200 individuals, and in 1980 it was", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8312565191996607, "w": 0.8264648534718486, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "confirmed females were also present. These birds have been the foundation of all", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8596116899500704, "w": 0.8261644553572005, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}, {"t": "subsequent work in managing the species.", "x": 0.10727675473252624, "y": 0.8879420400242081, "w": 0.4357241076048602, "h": 0.018822673091776602, "c": 1, "size": 13.0, "bold": false}], "source": "pdf text layer"}